= Arithmetic
{wiki}

Arithmetic studies elementary operations on numbers.

= Natural number
{title2=$\mathbb N$}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

The natural numbers are the nonnegative integers $0,1,2,\ldots$.

= Real number
{title2=$\mathbb R$}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

A real number is an element of the complete ordered field $\mathbb R$.

= Binary expansion
{parent=Real number}
{wiki=Binary_number}

Every real number in $[0,1]$ is a sum $\sum_{j\geq1}\varepsilon_j2^{-j}$ with binary digits $\varepsilon_j\in\{0,1\}$.

= Dyadic rational
{parent=Binary expansion}
{wiki}

A dyadic rational has the form $m/2^k$. Its terminating binary expansion has an alternative expansion ending in infinitely many $1$ digits.

= Extended real number
{title2=$\overline{\mathbb R}$}
{parent=Real number}
{wiki=Extended_real_number_line}

The extended real numbers adjoin $-\infty$ and $+\infty$ to $\mathbb R$. They preserve the <total order> but leave some arithmetic expressions, such as $+\infty-\infty$, undefined.

= Addition
{title2=$+$}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

Addition combines two numbers or elements of an additive algebraic structure into their sum.

= Additive inverse
{title2=$-x$}
{parent=Addition}
{wiki}

The additive inverse $-x$ satisfies $x+(-x)=0$.

= Sum
{title2=$\sum$}
{parent=Addition}
{wiki=Summation}

A sum combines a finite or infinite sequence of terms by repeated <addition>. The symbol $\sum$ denotes summation over an index.

= Weighted mean
{title2=$\bar x_w$}
{parent=Sum}
{wiki=Weighted_arithmetic_mean}

For positive weights $w_i$, the weighted mean is
$$
\bar x_w=\frac{\sum_iw_ix_i}{\sum_iw_i}.
$$
It lies between the smallest and largest $x_i$; the same result holds for integrals with a positive weight function.

= Arithmetic mean
{title2=$\bar x$}
{parent=Weighted mean}
{wiki}

The arithmetic mean of $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ is
$$
\bar x=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n x_i.
$$
It is the <weighted mean> with equal weights.

= Prefix sum
{title2=$P_m=\sum_{i=1}^m a_i$}
{parent=Sum}
{wiki}

The prefix sums $P_m=\sum_{i=1}^m a_i$ can all be computed by the recurrence $P_m=P_{m-1}+a_m$ in linear time. Any contiguous sum is then $P_b-P_{a-1}$, so it takes constant time after preprocessing.

= Subtraction
{title2=$-$}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

Subtraction adds the <additive inverse>: $a-b=a+(-b)$.

= Multiplication
{title2=$\times$}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

Multiplication combines two factors into their product.

= Multiplicative inverse
{title2=$x^{-1}$}
{parent=Multiplication}
{wiki}

A multiplicative inverse of $x$ is an element $x^{-1}$ satisfying $xx^{-1}=x^{-1}x=1$.

= Arithmetic progression
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki}

An arithmetic progression is a sequence $a,a+r,\ldots,a+(m-1)r$ with constant successive difference $r$. It is nonconstant when $r\ne0$.

= Common difference
{parent=Arithmetic progression}

The common difference of an <arithmetic progression> is the fixed difference between consecutive terms.

= Non-Archimedean analysis
{c}
{parent=Arithmetic}
{wiki=Non-Archimedean_ordered_field}

Non-Archimedean analysis studies fields whose distance is induced by an ultrametric absolute value, especially local fields and valued function fields.

= Absolute value on a field
{title2=$|\cdot|$}
{parent=Non-Archimedean analysis}
{wiki=Absolute_value_(algebra)}

An absolute value on a field is a nonnegative multiplicative function that vanishes only at zero and satisfies the triangle inequality.

= Absolute values on a field
{synonym}

= Non-Archimedean absolute value
{title2=$|\cdot|$}
{c}
{parent=Absolute value on a field}
{wiki=Non-Archimedean_ordered_field}

A non-Archimedean absolute value satisfies the ultrametric inequality $|x+y|\leq\max(|x|,|y|)$.

= Ultrametric inequality
{parent=Non-Archimedean absolute value}
{wiki=Ultrametric_space}

The ultrametric inequality is $d(x,z)\leq\max(d(x,y),d(y,z))$. For unequal $|x|$ and $|y|$, it implies $|x+y|=\max(|x|,|y|)$.

= Ultrametric space
{parent=Ultrametric inequality}
{wiki}

An ultrametric space is a metric space satisfying the ultrametric inequality. Its open balls are clopen, and any two balls are either disjoint or one contains the other.

= Totally disconnected space
{parent=Ultrametric space}
{wiki=Totally_disconnected_space}

A topological space is totally disconnected when its only connected subsets are singletons.

= Equivalent absolute values
{parent=Absolute value on a field}

Two nontrivial <absolute values on a field> are equivalent when they induce the same <topology>. Equivalently, one is a positive real power of the other.

= Extension of an absolute value
{parent=Absolute value on a field}

For a <field extension> $L/K$, an extension of an absolute value $|\cdot|$ on $K$ is an absolute value on $L$ whose restriction to $K$ is $|\cdot|$.

= Local factorization and extended absolute values
{parent=Extension of an absolute value}

If $L=K(\alpha)$ is a finite separable extension and $v$ is an absolute value on $K$, the extensions of $v$ to $L$ correspond to the irreducible factors of the minimal polynomial of $\alpha$ over the completion of $K$ at $v$.

= Hahn series field
{title2=$k((t^\Gamma))$}
{c}
{parent=Non-Archimedean analysis}
{wiki=Hahn_series}

For an ordered abelian group $\Gamma$, the Hahn series field $k((t^\Gamma))$ consists of formal sums with well-ordered support in $\Gamma$. The least exponent defines a valuation with value group $\Gamma$.

= p-adic absolute value
{title2=$|\cdot|_p$}
{parent=Non-Archimedean analysis}
{wiki=P-adic_number}

For a prime $p$, the $p$-adic absolute value on $\mathbb Q$ is $|x|_p=p^{-v_p(x)}$.

= Ostrowski theorem
{c}
{parent=p-adic absolute value}
{wiki=Ostrowski's_theorem}

Every nontrivial absolute value on $\mathbb Q$ is equivalent either to the usual absolute value or to one $p$-adic absolute value.

= Local field
{parent=Non-Archimedean analysis}
{wiki}

A local field is a nondiscrete locally compact topological field. A non-Archimedean local field is a finite extension of $\mathbb Q_p$ or of $\mathbb F_p((t))$.

= Hensel lemma
{c}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki=Hensel's_lemma}

For a complete discrete valuation ring $R$, a simple root of a polynomial modulo the maximal ideal lifts uniquely to a root in $R$.

= Laurent series field
{title2=$k((t))$}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki=Formal_power_series#Formal_Laurent_series}

The Laurent series field $k((t))$ consists of formal series $\sum_{n\geq N}a_nt^n$. Its $t$-adic valuation is the least exponent with nonzero coefficient.

= p-adic logarithm
{title2=$\log_p$}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki=P-adic_exponential_function}

The $p$-adic logarithm is the convergent series $\log(1+x)=\sum_{n\geq1}(-1)^{n+1}x^n/n$ on a sufficiently small group of principal units.

= p-adic exponential
{title2=$\exp_p$}
{parent=p-adic logarithm}
{wiki=P-adic_exponential_function}

The $p$-adic exponential $\exp(x)=\sum_{n\geq0}x^n/n!$ converges near zero and is locally inverse to the $p$-adic logarithm.

= Teichmuller representative
{c}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki=Teichmüller_character}

The Teichmuller representative of a nonzero residue class $\bar a\in\mathbb F_q^*$ is the unique lift $\omega$ satisfying $\omega^{q-1}=1$ and $\omega\equiv a$ modulo the maximal ideal.

= Unramified extension
{parent=Local field}
{wiki=Unramified_extension}

A finite extension of local fields is unramified when its ramification index is one and its residue-field degree equals the field degree. A local field has a unique unramified extension of each positive degree inside a fixed algebraic closure.

= Ramification group
{title2=$G_i$}
{parent=Unramified extension}
{wiki=Ramification_group}

For a finite Galois extension of local fields, the lower ramification groups measure how closely automorphisms fix the valuation ring. The inertia group is $G_0$, and $G_1$ is the wild inertia group.

= Ramification groups
{synonym}

= Inertia group
{title2=$G_0$}
{parent=Ramification group}
{wiki}

The inertia group of a finite <Galois extension> of local fields is the kernel of the action of its <Galois group> on the residue field. In lower numbering it is the zeroth <ramification group> $G_0$.

= Wild inertia group
{title2=$G_1$}
{parent=Inertia group}
{wiki}

The wild inertia group is the first <ramification group> $G_1$. It is the unique Sylow subgroup of the <inertia group> for the residue characteristic.

= Eisenstein polynomial
{c}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki}

For a <discrete valuation ring> with <uniformizer> $\pi$, an Eisenstein polynomial is a monic polynomial whose nonleading coefficients are divisible by $\pi$ and whose constant coefficient is not divisible by $\pi^2$. It is <irreducible polynomial>[irreducible], and adjoining one of its roots gives a totally ramified extension.

= Newton polygon
{parent=Local field}
{wiki}

The Newton polygon of a polynomial over a valued field is the lower convex hull of the points formed from coefficient indices and valuations. Its slopes determine the valuations of roots, counted with multiplicity.

= Ramification index
{title2=$e(L/K)$}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki}

For a finite extension $L/K$ of non-Archimedean local fields, the ramification index $e(L/K)$ is the index of the value group of $K$ in that of $L$. Normalized valuations satisfy $v_L|_K=e(L/K)v_K$.

= Residue-field degree
{title2=$f(L/K)$}
{parent=Ramification index}

The residue-field degree of a finite extension of local fields is $f(L/K)=[k_L:k_K]$, and $[L:K]=e(L/K)f(L/K)$.

= Totally ramified extension
{parent=Ramification index}
{wiki}

A finite extension of local fields is totally ramified when its <residue-field degree> is one, equivalently when its <ramification index> equals its field degree.

= p-adic unit
{title2=$\mathbb Z_p^\times$}
{parent=Local field}

A p-adic unit is an element of $\mathbb Z_p^\times$, equivalently a p-adic integer of valuation zero.

= Principal unit
{parent=p-adic unit}
{wiki}

For a non-Archimedean local field $K$ with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m_K$, a principal unit is an element of $1+\mathfrak m_K$. The higher principal-unit groups are $1+\mathfrak m_K^n$.

= Roots of unity in a p-adic field
{title2=$\mu(K)$}
{parent=Local field}

The roots of unity in a finite extension $K/\mathbb Q_p$ form a finite group. Each element of their prime-to-$p$ part is a <Teichmuller representative>, while any $p$-power part lies among the <principal units>.

= Maximal abelian extension
{title2=$K^{\mathrm{ab}}$}
{parent=Local field}
{wiki}

The maximal abelian extension $K^{\mathrm{ab}}$ of a field $K$ is the compositum in a fixed separable closure of all finite abelian extensions of $K$.

= Weil group
{title2=$W_K$}
{c}
{parent=Maximal abelian extension}
{wiki}

For a non-Archimedean local field $K$, the Weil group is the inverse image of the infinite cyclic subgroup generated by Frobenius under $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline K/K)\to\operatorname{Gal}(\overline k/k)\cong\widehat{\mathbb Z}$. It is dense in the absolute Galois group with its profinite topology.

= Cyclotomic extension of a p-adic field
{parent=Maximal abelian extension}

The cyclotomic extension $\mathbb Q_p(\zeta_{p^n})/\mathbb Q_p$ is generated by a primitive $p^n$th root of unity. It is totally ramified and has Galois group $(\mathbb Z/p^n\mathbb Z)^\times$.

= Local Kronecker-Weber theorem
{c}
{parent=Maximal abelian extension}

The local Kronecker-Weber theorem states that every finite abelian extension of $\mathbb Q_p$ is contained in an extension obtained by adjoining roots of unity.

= Local class field theory
{parent=Maximal abelian extension}
{wiki}

Local class field theory describes the abelian extensions of a local field $K$ through its multiplicative group $K^\times$.

= Local Artin reciprocity
{c}
{parent=Local class field theory}

Local Artin reciprocity gives a continuous homomorphism $\operatorname{Art}_K:K^\times\to\operatorname{Gal}(K^{\mathrm{ab}}/K)$ with dense image. For every finite abelian extension $L/K$, it induces an isomorphism
$$
K^\times/N_{L/K}(L^\times)\cong\operatorname{Gal}(L/K).
$$

= Local Artin map
{title2=$\operatorname{Art}_K$}
{c}
{parent=Local Artin reciprocity}
{wiki=Artin_reciprocity_law}

The local Artin map is the reciprocity homomorphism of <local Artin reciprocity>. Its normalization is fixed by choosing whether a <uniformizer> maps to arithmetic or geometric Frobenius.

= Norm subgroup of a local field extension
{title2=$N_{L/K}(L^\times)$}
{parent=Local Artin reciprocity}

For a finite extension of local fields $L/K$, its norm subgroup is the image of the <field norm> $N_{L/K}:L^\times\to K^\times$. For an abelian extension, it is the kernel of the induced local Artin map to $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$.
